Wait, the stub's fetch is `global.fetch = async (url) => {...}` — throwing inside async = rejected promise. My `await fetch(...)` in try/catch → caught. Good. So in smoke tests: loadSkin safely returns null without needing import() at all if I probe with fetch FIRST and return before import when probe fails. That's exactly my manifest-probe design: probe 404/throw → return null → import() never runs → no Function-constructor import issues in the greek smoke test. 

For my GVG test (to be written), I'll stub fetch to return {ok:true} for the skin manifest probe, but then skin.js must actually load... my test could pre-register the skin module differently — hmm, how would the GVG test load skin.js? If import() inside new Function body fails in Node, my test can't exercise the dynamic import path. Options: loadSkin accepts an injection point: `window.__mythicSkinOverride` (or globalThis) — the test sets `global.__gvgSkin = (await import(...)).default` before loading app.js? But loadSkin decides based on probe + import... I can structure loadSkin:

